\noindent
\textbf{\textcolor{Accent}{Developing activity--travel schedules requires several interdependent decisions --- activity participation, duration, timing, destination, and mode choice --- shaped by individual characteristics, spatial context, and knowledge of the transport environment. Among these influences, safety plays a critical role: travellers may avoid certain modes, choose safer routes, or avoid areas altogether, even when less safe alternatives offer shorter travel times. Conventional activity-based models (ABMs) typically encode safety only implicitly, treating crash risk as a network outcome rather than a behavioural input. We introduce a hierarchical ABM for South-East Queensland (SEQ) in which expected crash frequency enters explicitly as a utility component across the relevant decision layers. The framework generates full daily activity--travel plans for a synthetic population of 3,820,161 residents across 371 Statistical Area Level 2 (SA2) zones, producing origin--destination flows suitable for dynamic network assignment. Coordinated daily activity pattern classification, activity frequency, day-duration budgeting, per-activity duration, schedule placement, and joint mode--destination choice are estimated on Queensland Household Travel Survey (HHTS) microdata and applied to the full synthetic population. Safety terms derived from crash-prediction functions enter the mode- and destination-choice utilities alongside travel time, distance, and cost. An evolutionary feature-selection procedure (SparseEA-AGDS) replaces analyst-driven variable specification by conducting significance-constrained total-BIC hypothesis testing across all estimation stages --- over covariate masks, random-parameter status, and functional-form selectors --- identifying the person attributes and network characteristics most influential in each decision layer. Controlled experiments comparing the safety-integrated model against a baseline lacking safety skims quantify how crash-risk exposure reshapes mode shares and destination patterns. The search selects crash-exposure terms alongside conventional predictors, though individual-coefficient precision within the HHTS sample is limited; we report significant and non-significant outcomes transparently and distinguish selection (BIC) evidence from coefficient significance. Methodologically, the paper contributes to choice modelling through a nested mode--destination structure with logsum linkage, sampling-of-alternatives estimation with sampling correction, tested choice-set refinements (parametrised-logit captivity, schedule-constrained sets), random-regret versus random-utility timing, and systematic treatment of observed and unobserved heterogeneity under significance-constrained specification search. This article reports the full estimation, benchmark reproduction, controlled safety experiments, and full synthetic-population application. Because the specifications are found by search, every searched quantity is subjected to post-selection inference --- sample splitting, conditional inference, and a search-aware parametric bootstrap --- while the pre-specified safety terms and the counterfactual comparisons require no correction. Safety is therefore evaluated not on penalised fit but on behaviour: mode and spatial substitution, active-mode accessibility, distributional response across risk-sensitive groups, and the induced active-travel elasticity of a crash-reduction scenario. All validation is held out: no selection-side quantity is reported as a validation figure.}}
