\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Activity-based travel demand models forecast how individuals organise time across activities and trips, informing infrastructure investment, land-use planning, and road safety policy (\Cref{fig:pipeline}). The argument developed here is that road safety is not merely an outcome to be audited after the model is run; it is a component of the behavioural utility that produces travel patterns in the first place. Conventional frameworks fit demand on travel time, cost and socio-economic characteristics, then diagnose crash exposure ex post. A safety-augmented framework instead lets expected crash frequency inform those same choices, so that the model reproduces the avoidance, substitution and route/destination responses that travellers already make when safety matters.

A closely related problem is empirical identification: with 30 stereotype--type crash pairs, no closed-form variable set transfers from one road context to another. The comparison the paper evaluates is fixed by three formalised programmes in Materials and Methods: (P0), the pure-ActivitySim baseline fitted normally --- published specifications, universal choice sets, plain maximum likelihood; (P1), the crash-prediction model search; and (P2), the activity-based specification search. P1 exposes the identification problem by searching covariates, transformations and mixing laws under BIC subject to a significance floor, returning a parsimonious specification supported by hypothesis testing rather than analyst judgment. Applied to SEQ and compared against the fixed (P0) baseline (\Cref{eq:p0}) with universal choice sets, the searched safety terms enter the utility alongside the conventional attributes and are tested, not assumed.

 The activity-program generation view \citep{Bhat1993ActivityProgram} and complex travel behaviour theory \citep{Recker1986ComplexTravel} motivate the hierarchical decision structure adopted here. Yet almost none of these frameworks treat safety as a factor that shapes demand: in conventional ABMs, safety is computed \emph{after} demand is assigned, so mode, destination, and route choice are blind to crash exposure \citep{Bowman2001Activity-BasedSchedules}.

