This omission is consequential. Empirical studies consistently find that perceived and actual crash risk influence mode selection (stated-choice practice more broadly reviewed in \citep{BekkerGrobRyanGerard2012Review}), active-travel uptake, departure time, and joint versus independent travel \citep{Gossling2024ChildrensSpace,Swain2024ChildrensTravel,Duren2023IdentifyingComponents,Huang2025YoungApproach,Tzouras2025ExploringChoices}. Those most affected --- children, the elderly, low-income workers --- are least able to absorb the cost of unsafe infrastructure through mode substitution, so omitting safety systematically biases forecasts against the most risk-sensitive travellers. A parallel barrier is data fragmentation: implementing activity-based models is constrained less by modelling capability than by the availability of interoperable, behaviourally rich datasets spanning behavioural, spatial, temporal, and system dimensions \citep{Perera2026Data}.

To address potential covariate correlation we embed a preliminary structural-equation-model (SEM) step before variable selection. The SEM quantifies each candidate covariate's latent (common-factor) component against its measured part: estimated loadings and residuals are retained, and any covariate whose variance is substantially absorbed by a latent factor --- i.e.\ whose behavioural variation is largely unobserved heterogeneity rather than observable measurement --- is flagged as a candidate for a random-parameter (mixing) specification. The flag is advisory, not decisive: the model form of each flagged covariate is fixed \emph{ex ante} only where imposed by structural constraints, while the random-parameter status itself is a searched gene $\mathbf{r}_s$ \eqref{eq:c-link}, so the SEM decides candidacy and the search, priced by total BIC, decides adoption. The step runs automatically before every estimation stage, and its diagnostics (loadings, residuals, flags) are saved alongside the search logs (see the software appendix). For specification completeness: candidate mixing laws are Normal, Uniform, Log-normal, Triangular, and Truncated-Normal (\Cref{tab:notation}), integration is by Halton draws with every stochastic element drawn from fixed per-segment seeds recorded in the run manifest, so any converged fit reproduces bit for bit; random-parameter variance estimates and the associated heterogeneity diagnostics are reported wherever a random parameter is selected, and heterogeneity interacting with crash sensitivity is priced through the same searched genes (the crash term itself is force-included, not mixed).

The paper makes a methodological contribution and presents an innovative application of choice analysis. Its contribution is the treatment of safety inside a linked activity--travel system: two-level nested tour mode choice with mode logsums entering destination utilities, Manski--Lerman sampling of the 371-zone destination universe with sampling correction, tested refinements to universal choice sets (parametrised-logit captivity (PLC) \citep{SwaitBenAkiva1987PLC}, planning-constrained (M3) schedule sets \citep{Auld2011PlanningConstrained}, and a machine-learned consideration-set arm following \citet{Volakakis2025IntegratingChoice,Yao2022VAE,Phan2022NewApproach}), random-regret versus random-utility schedule placement \citep{Golshani2018ActivityModels}, mixed-logit heterogeneity where identified, and orthogonalised crash-exposure terms that separate crash sensitivity from distance. The specification search is framed as choice-model selection under significance-reporting concerns \citep{Hess2026Significance}: BIC-led search with explicit significance constraints, likelihood-ratio tests for forced safety terms, and transparent reporting of non-significant outcomes. The paper also contributes to methodological advances, revealed-preference data integration (HHTS microdata fused with network, crash, and census sources at full-population scale), behavioural insights (safety as perceived and actual risk shaping mode, destination, timing, and participation), and policy evaluation for risk-sensitive travellers who cannot easily substitute away from unsafe conditions. Our treatment of preference heterogeneity follows the consumer-heterogeneity tradition \citep{AllenbyRossi1998Heterogeneity,HessRose2009Intra}, our sampling-and-efficiency discussion follows \citep{BliemerRoseHess2008Approx,RoseBliemerHensherCollins2008Designing}, and our willingness-to-pay caution follows the decision-versus-analyst-error and stated-choice-review lessons \citep{BekkerGrobRoseBliemer2013Closer,BekkerGrobRyanGerard2012Review}, including recent health-modelling evidence \citep{BekkerGrobHole2024Editorial}. The paper presents the complete six-stage estimation, the P0-versus-P2 mathematical-program comparison, Exampville and prototype-MTC transfer tests, controlled $\mathcal{M}_S$-versus-$\mathcal{M}_0$ safety experiments, and the 3.8-million-person SEQ application.

We address both barriers with three contributions. \textbf{First}, a safety-augmented six-stage hierarchical ABM for SEQ in which crash-frequency safety skims enter the utility functions directly: expected crash frequency, derived from Empirical Bayes--adjusted crash-prediction models calibrated on the Queensland road network, enters both mode-choice and destination-choice utilities, and a home-zone crash-exposure accessibility measure is supplied to the upstream decision layers. \textbf{Second}, an evolutionary feature-selection procedure that replaces analyst-imposed variable specifications across all estimation stages: a BIC-guided search over binary feature masks, executed by the SparseEA-AGDS engine (adaptive genetic operators with dynamic gene scoring), systematically identifies which person attributes, network characteristics, and safety terms influence each decision layer. \textbf{Third}, controlled experiments (the safety-integrated model $\mathcal{M}_S$ compared against the baseline $\mathcal{M}_0$ lacking safety skims) and a full synthetic-population scale-up ($\sim$3.8~million persons, $\sim$7.1~million tours, 371 zones) enabling zone-level policy evaluation and network assignment that survey data alone cannot support. The paper's central comparison, formalised as mathematical programs in Materials and Methods, is between a pure-ActivitySim baseline fitted normally --- published specifications, universal choice sets, plain maximum likelihood --- and the searched alternative: the same estimators, feeding, and calibration, with every modelling decision in each stage subjected to hypothesis testing. Because both arms share machinery, any error reduction is attributable to the tested decisions rather than to effort or calibration. Consistent with the choice-set-generation evidence synthesised for this revision, choice-set formation is treated here as a behavioural design choice rather than a computational convenience: universal sets are the P0 baseline, while P2 tests individualised, schedule-conditioned, captivity-aware, and learned consideration sets, retaining a refinement only when it reduces BIC by more than 2 and improves validation. %\\textbf{Related work and literature review.} The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section~2 reviews relevant literature on activity‑based modelling, safety in travel behaviour, and automated feature selection. Section~3 describes the six‑stage ABM structure and the synthetic‑population scale‑up. Section~4 presents the behavioural model forms. Section~5 details the SA‑bandit feature‑selection framework. Section~6 describes the safety experiment design, including the controlled comparison, metrics, and reproducibility. Section~7 summarises the data sources. Section~8 concludes. Appendices provide the full SA operator definitions, candidate variable pools, ABS coding tables, skim alias mappings, and the trip record schema. Notation is collated once in \Cref{tab:notation} (indices for persons, households, tours, legs, modes, zones, segments, and links) and referenced thereafter.

