This section situates the paper in three literatures: activity-based travel demand modelling as the behavioural framework, safety --- perceived and objective --- as a determinant of travel behaviour and hence a utility component rather than a downstream outcome, and systematic feature selection as the alternative to analyst-driven specification across the multi-stage ABM.

\subsection{Activity-Based Travel Demand Models}


Activity-based models (ABMs) simulate the full sequence of activities and trips that individuals undertake throughout the day, providing a behaviourally grounded representation of travel demand. Rather than treating trips as isolated events, ABMs conceptualise travel as a derived demand that emerges from individuals’ need to participate in activities distributed across space and time. This framework is aligned with the broader behavioral sciences, which emphasize that people organize their daily lives around participation in activities, with travel serving as the mechanism that enables access to opportunities \citep{Bowman2001Activity-BasedSchedules, Ben-Akiva1985DiscreteDemand}.
A central strength of ABMs is their explicit representation of temporal and spatial constraints \citep{Board2014Activity-BasedPrimer}. Individuals face fixed commitments (e.g., work start times), flexible activities (e.g., shopping), and discretionary opportunities (e.g., recreation), all of which must be arranged within a finite daily time budget. ABMs capture these constraints by modeling activity timing, duration, and sequencing, thereby producing schedules that reflect realistic trade-offs between competing demands. Trip‑based models, by design, do not capture the interdependencies between trips or the cumulative influence of earlier decisions on later ones. Several efforts have attempted to extend or adapt trip‑based frameworks to incorporate some of these dynamics. For example, \citet{Bernardin2009EnhancedCompetition} demonstrate that destination‑choice models can yield significant improvements when they account for agglomeration effects and competition between destinations, thereby partially complementing trip‑based formulations. However, these enhancements still fall short of representing the full sequence‑dependent nature of travel behavior that activity‑based models are designed to capture with tour-based and scheduled based representations.



ABMs also incorporate heterogeneity at the person and household level. Differences in income, employment status, household composition, vehicle availability, and accessibility shape the feasibility and attractiveness of different activity–travel patterns. By modeling individuals rather than aggregate flows, ABMs can represent distributional effects, equity impacts, and behavioral responses that vary across demographic groups. This makes them particularly well suited for evaluating policies such as congestion pricing, land-use changes, or investments in active transport infrastructure.
There is also much more pressing research in joint travels, travels are not just shaped by individual preferences, but shared activities are likely to occur \citep{VandenBerg2012ADuration}.
Crucially, \citet{Perera2026Data} demonstrate that advancing from four-step models to
AcBMs fundamentally restructures data requirements: rather than aggregate trip rates
and OD matrices, AcBMs require disaggregate datasets that are behaviourally rich,
temporally explicit, spatially consistent, and interoperable across modelling
components.  This reframing of AcBM implementation as a data integration problem is
directly relevant to the present study, in which behavioural survey data (HHTS),
synthetic population attributes (ABS Census), network skims, and crash-frequency
predictions must be reconciled across common spatial units and estimation stages.




The theoretical case for full joint estimation of all activity-travel decisions is well
established \citep{Walker2002GeneralizedModel}, but practical implementations almost
universally adopt sequential or hierarchical structures. Full joint estimation of complete
daily schedules faces a combinatorial explosion of alternatives, sparse observation
(most travel surveys provide one or two diary days per person), and extreme computational
demands \citep{Kim2016ACars, Ren2022ASet}. As a result, modular sequential frameworks ---
exemplified by ActivitySim and Atom ---
are standard \citep{Board2014Activity-BasedPrimer}. Even nominally joint
formulations decompose decisions during prediction \citep{Nayak2023ANetworks}, reinforcing that
sequential conditional logic is unavoidable at metropolitan scale.

Error propagation across sequential stages is a recognized limitation. Small
misspecifications in destination or mode choice can compound and cascade through the schedule,  affecting network-level flows and congestion patterns 
\citep{Gibbons2025EvaluatingModel}. To mitigate this, most ABMs incorporate a feedback mechanism in which congested travel times from network assignment are fed back into the demand model, approximating a fixed-point equilibrium without the instability associated with tightly coupled formulations \citep{Gemma2022CaseAssignment}. The present framework follows this convention, employing a six-stage sequential model with skim-feedback capability to ensure consistency between predicted travel patterns and network performance.


\citet{Dharmowijoyo2014Day-To-DayCountry} focused on the activity–travel behaviour of individuals; their analysis indicated that compared to workers and students, non-workers were making fewer trips, had a lower dependency on personalized modes, were involved in a smaller number of trip chains, and were allocating less time for travelling.








\subsection{Safety as a Behavioral Determinant}
Individuals with different demographic characteristics such as  age, gender, ethnicity, working class, among many others, often have different attitudes towards their environment which can alter their perceived response to safety \citep{Qin2025TheReview}. Some research has highlighted the important contribution of work-related dimensions in transportation studies, which have often been neglected when adopting best practices to mitigate work-related issues. Work-related psychology can be heavily affected by travel factors, and addressing these factors can promote safer commuting, especially since a large proportion of travel is work-related \citep{Tokat2026FromSafety}.
 Walking and cycling are the most studied mobility modes, as individuals are fully exposed to the urban environment, making their perceptions highly influenced by built environment features. Driving involves more limited exposure. Notably, even on the same road, pedestrians and drivers may experience different safety perceptions.

Safety is a fundamental determinant of travel behavior, shaping how individuals choose modes, routes, and travel together. Demographic factors interact strongly with safety outcomes, particularly for active transport modes such as walking and cycling, where pedestrian and bicyclist crashes are central to statewide planning and project prioritization \citep{Bian2025DevelopingData}. A substantial body of evidence shows that travelers routinely adjust their mobility decisions in response to perceived injury risk \citep{Mladenovic2014TheProcess, Roorda2008ValidationModel, Luan2025AnalyzingModel}.
Children’s independent mobility is especially sensitive to traffic danger: parents often suppress walking and cycling and instead substitute car escort when they perceive the environment as unsafe \citep{Gossling2024ChildrensSpace, Swain2024ChildrensTravel}. Among young adults, active transport uptake is similarly moderated by safety culture and infrastructure quality \citep{Huang2025YoungApproach}. Safety concerns also extend to public transport use, where women may be deterred by fears of harassment or conflict‑provoking interactions \citep{Alimo2026TransactionalSatisfaction}. Perceptions of safety are further shaped by visual cues in the built environment---such as enclosure, greenery, visibility, and the presence or absence of walls, fences, and buildings---which influence how streets feel and can either attract or deter mobility.
Despite these well‑documented behavioral responses, mainstream activity‑based models (ABMs) typically treat safety only as a network performance metric rather than as a component of individual decision utility. Models that omit safety cannot capture the real-world adjustments travelers make when confronted with unsafe conditions.
A practical pathway to integrating safety into ABMs is through Safety Performance Functions (SPFs), which translate network geometry, speed, and traffic volume into expected crash frequencies at the link level \citep{Fang2024APrediction, Bartin2023SafetySegments}. As travelers move across these links, they accumulate crash exposure as part of their daily travel patterns. Aggregating expected crashes along the routes implied by origin–destination movements yields OD‑level safety measures. Incorporating these aggregated crash‑exposure metrics into utility functions allows expected crashes to influence travel decisions in the same manner as travel time, distance, and monetary costs. This is the approach adopted in this study.

Built‑environment research further underscores the need for safety‑conscious modeling. Areas with higher proportions of industrial, commercial, and open land uses tend to exhibit elevated pedestrian crash probabilities, whereas residential areas show significantly lower likelihoods. Locations with more schools and transit stops—key generators of pedestrian activity—also experience increased crash incidence \citep{Ukkusuri2012TheFrequency}. Urban functional zoning, population distribution, and road network characteristics are strongly correlated with pedestrian crash frequency and severity. Increases in social services and activity intensity contribute to complex pedestrian–vehicle interactions, heightening conflict risk. Mixed‑use commercial–residential areas, which generate and attract substantial traffic throughout the day, require particular attention in planning and project implementation, consistent with previous findings \citep{Pulugurtha2013TrafficCharacteristics, Chen2016EffectsRisk}. These insights support safety‑conscious planning, land‑use decisions, long‑range transportation plans, and proactive deployment of safety treatments in high‑risk pedestrian crash networks \citep{Chen2021TheOhio}.
From a macro‑planning perspective, intersection design, road network density, and road mileage should be evaluated alongside population density and the distribution of social services and activities. Higher pedestrian crash frequencies are consistently associated with larger populations, denser road networks, and medium‑intensity land use \citep{Wang2016Macro-levelChina}.
More broadly, rapid urbanization, population growth, and motorization challenge the ability of current transport systems to meet mobility needs sustainably. Areas exposed to increased traffic crashes and congestion increase travel times, fuel consumption, and carbon emissions, undermining both efficiency and sustainability \citep{Ahmad2023ApplicationsReview}. Integrating safety into transportation network modeling is, therefore, not only a behavioral necessity but also a prerequisite for sustainable mobility planning.

\subsection{Feature Selection in Activity-Based Models}

Parameter estimation in activity-based models has conventionally relied on analyst
judgment to specify which individual attributes, land-use variables, and network skims
enter each sub-model's utility function.  The analyst faces a modelling spiral:
variables are selected, functional forms are specified, models are estimated and
evaluated, predictions are validated, and the cycle repeats---sometimes across six or
more interdependent stages, each containing multiple model segments.  With candidate
pools of 20--50 variables per stage and hundreds of possible functional-form
variants, the analyst cannot test everything.  This practice introduces subjectivity,
risks omitted-variable bias, and transfers poorly, particularly when models are
dependent on each other \citep{Beeramole2024SearchlogitPyPI}.

A direct precursor to the present study is the MetaCountRegressor package of
\citet{Ahern2023MetacountregressorPyPI} and \citet{Ahern2024ExtensiveModels}, which
automates the selection of count-data specifications (Poisson, negative binomial,
random-parameter variants) via simulated-annealing search guided by the Bayesian
Information Criterion (BIC).  That work established that the same BIC objective,
the same constraint architecture, and the same SA engine could be applied uniformly
across disparate model classes to discover parsimonious, well-fitting
specifications without analyst specification bias.  The present study extends this
unified search paradigm from count models to the full activity-based pipeline: every
stage from daily-activity-pattern classification through mode and destination choice
is specified by the same BIC-guided SA-bandit procedure, operating under the same
constraint architecture.  Variable selection using information criteria
within evolutionary or wrapper-based search
procedures offers a systematic alternative to analyst-driven specification, allowing
the modeller to test everything that is deemed relevant upfront
\citep{Rahnasto2024ComparingChoice, Mishra2018EstimationApplications}.
The present study utilizes a simulated annealing to discover a set of parsimonious
models across the entire ABM by maximizing model fit according to BIC subject to
constraints. The SA framework accommodates analyst-imposed constraints, such as
requiring specific variables to appear in the functional form while also providing
flexibility to test alternative specifications. This extensive hypothesis-driven
search strategy has been applied previously to individual choice models and count
models, but it is particularly advantageous in a framework that requires multiple
interdependent decisions across nested behavioral stages
\citep{Ahern2024ExtensiveModels, Paz2015CalibrationAlgorithm}.

\section{Materials and Methods}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Six-stage activity--travel pipeline, validated as a complete plan}
The framework generates full daily plans for the SEQ synthetic population (3,820,161 persons across 371 SA2 zones --- 370 populated; the Lake Manchester--England Creek reservoir catchment has neither residents nor jobs; 113 fringe zones sit outside the EMME network) through six sequential stages, each estimated on HHTS microdata ($\sim$20,000 persons; 23,189 for CDAP) and applied to every synthetic person (\Cref{fig:pipeline}). Stage outputs feed forward as inputs; a skim-feedback capability keeps predicted patterns consistent with network performance. End-to-end runtime is 17h39m ($\sim$12.5h estimation plus application; peak memory 188\,GB), dominated by Stage~1 (11h49m) and Stage~4 (5h07m), with Stage~5 fitting in 37m30s --- establishing that full-population safety-augmented ABM estimation and application is feasible on institutional HPC rather than merely in principle.

\subsection{Safety hypothesis and the crash-frequency chain in brief}
The hypothesis tested throughout this paper is that safety is a behavioural input rather than an ex-post outcome: expected crash frequency belongs inside the utilities that generate travel, and the feature search decides where it survives. The safety terms arming those utilities are produced by a five-step crash-frequency chain running alongside --- not inside --- the six behavioural stages. Queensland open crash records are conflated onto $100\,$m homogeneous road segments stratified by road stereotype and crash type (step~1); SPF-style count models are estimated per stereotype--type context by the paper's simulated-annealing search (step~2); the fitted models predict segment frequencies (step~3); predictions are blended with observation under Empirical Bayes (EB) (step~4); and expected crashes are transferred to network links and accumulated along inter-zonal paths into the $371\times371$ \texttt{CRASH} skim consumed by Stage~5 (step~5). The chain is complete, and its outputs are the artefacts the behavioural stages consume: segment-level expected counts, per-zone Empirical Bayes predictions --- which supply the home-zone crash-accessibility index offered to the participation layer --- and the OD \texttt{CRASH} skim (\Cref{fig:pipeline}, right rail). Construction and estimation detail are reported in \Cref{sec:crash-workflow}; the behavioural value of the crash terms is adjudicated by the $\mathcal{M}_S$-versus-$\mathcal{M}_0$ experiments of \Cref{sec:experiments}.

\paragraph{From records to per-zone risk.} The Queensland network is categorised into six road stereotypes (rural undivided, rural remote, urban undivided, urban divided, urban local, divided freeways/highways) crossed with five crash types (run-off-road, head-on, rear-end, intersection, other); 28 of the 30 stereotype--type combinations are estimable. Each estimable pair is fitted by the same simulated-annealing search used for every model in this paper --- Poisson, negative-binomial, and random-parameter families with flexible covariate transformations and mixing laws --- selected by BIC subject to a significance floor (\Cref{tab:sa_config}). Empirical Bayes shrinkage \eqref{eq:eb} stabilises each segment, preserving aggregate crash totals while collapsing per-segment dispersion, and the expected counts aggregate to the per-zone crash-risk index read by the participation layer.

\paragraph{From per-zone risk to the OD skim.} Expected crashes are mapped from $100\,$m segments to network links with lane-proportional shares, and distance-weighted shortest paths between all 371 SA2 zones ($137\mbox{,}641$ ordered pairs) accumulate them by crash type and in total, $\mathrm{CRASH}_{jk}=\sum_{\ell\in R_{jk}}\mathrm{EB}_\ell$ \eqref{eq:crash-od}, yielding the skim of \Cref{tab:od_sample}.

\paragraph{Counts to behaviour.} The count model per stereotype--type pair is the (C0) maximisation program \eqref{eq:c0}: family and covariate decisions under BIC and significance, with Empirical Bayes shrinkage \eqref{eq:eb} stabilising each segment before lane-proportional transfer and OD accumulation. The resulting matrix is what makes safety priced: $\log(\mathrm{CRASH}_{jk}+1)$ enters destination utility orthogonalised on log-distance \eqref{eq:ortho}, mode logsums carry mode-level crash disutility into spatial demand, and a home-zone crash-accessibility index is offered to the upstream participation layer. Whether these terms survive the search is exactly what the $\mathcal{M}_S$-versus-$\mathcal{M}_0$ experiments adjudicate.

\subsection{Stage 0 --- Coordinated daily activity pattern (CDAP)}
 A three-alternative multinomial logit over Home / Non-mandatory-only / Mandatory (H/N/M), with 18 selected person, household, accessibility, and census-bridge features:
\begin{equation}
P_i(d)=\frac{e^{V_{id}}}{\sum_{d'\in\{H,N,M\}}e^{V_{id'}}},\qquad
V_{id}=\boldsymbol{\beta}_d^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{n(i)},\quad d\in\{H,N,M\},
\label{eq:m0}
\end{equation}
The multinomial logit is the workhorse discrete-choice form \citep{Ben-Akiva1985DiscreteDemand}; mixing over taste parameters captures intra-person heterogeneity where identified \citep{HessRose2009Intra,AllenbyRossi1998Heterogeneity}. A mixed-logit refinement is tested where the estimation arm enables it, replacing $\boldsymbol{\beta}_d$ by a random draw from the searched mixing law \citep{Walker2002GeneralizedModel}. The selected individual specification conditions downstream activity generation. The optional household layer estimates a joint household DAP pattern by household size \citep{Xu2018RandomUtility},
\begin{equation}
P(\mathbf{d}_h)=\frac{\exp\!\bigl(\sum_{n\in\mathcal{I}_h}V_{nd_n}+I(\mathbf{d}_h)\bigr)}
{\sum_{\mathbf{d}'}\exp\!\bigl(\sum_{n\in\mathcal{I}_h}V_{nd'_n}+I(\mathbf{d}')\bigr)},\qquad
I(\cdot)\ \text{2--5-way household interactions},
\label{eq:m0-joint}
\end{equation}
and then joint-tour frequency, participation, and purpose, so joint tours and shared escorts are generated structurally rather than being carried only as observed person attributes \citep{Xu2018RandomUtility}. Prediction loads the saved joint fit instead of heuristic priors, draws members jointly from their size's tables, and persists coordinated patterns per person (\texttt{CDAP}, household pattern \texttt{CDAP\_HH\_PATTERN}, \texttt{CDAP\_COORDINATED}) for every downstream stage; the estimation-side counterpart attaches the same vocabulary to HHTS deterministically (observed \texttt{cdap\_M/N/H}, composition, joint counts). Individual interaction coefficients are individually weak ($|t|<1.3$); the contribution is structural (household-coherent draws).

\subsection{Stage 1 --- Activity frequency}
Per-purpose, per-person-type \emph{count} models (Poisson / negative binomial; ordered logit serves only as the structural baseline), covering work, education, social, escort, shopping, other maintenance, other discretionary, and eating out ($\sim$16 features per segment):
\begin{equation}
y_{ip}\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{ip})\ \ \text{or}\ \ \mathrm{NB}(\lambda_{ip},\alpha),\qquad
\ln\lambda_{ip}=V_{ip}=\mathbf{x}_{ip}(G)'\boldsymbol{\theta},
\label{eq:m1}
\end{equation}
with the structural baseline given by the ordered-logit form
\begin{equation}
P(y_{ip}\le c)=\Lambda(\tau_c-V_{ip}),\qquad c=0,1,\dots,
\label{eq:m1ol}
\end{equation}
The count-model search is the one used for crash-frequency estimation \citep{Ahern2024ExtensiveModels,Ahern2023MetacountregressorPyPI}, applied here to activity counts that carry the same excess-of-zeros and overdispersion structure identified in Australian count data \citep{Behara2021AQueensland}. Household interaction in in-home and out-of-home maintenance participation \citep{Srinivasan2005ModelingParticipation} and multi-day participation patterns \citep{Cho2023NeedBased} motivate the household covariates entering $G$. Ordered-logit thresholds carry delta-method standard errors (a prior exporter omission, now fixed --- not an estimator problem). Mandatory guardrails prevent impossible zero mandatory-activity counts for full-time workers, school children, and full-time tertiary students.

\subsection{Stage 2 --- Day duration budget}
Censored regressions for start-of-day and end-of-day plus a lognormal budget regression for total out-of-home time $B$:
\begin{equation}
\ln B_i=V_i+\sigma\varepsilon_i,\qquad
\varepsilon_i\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1),\qquad V_i=\mathbf{x}_i(G)'\boldsymbol{\theta},
\label{eq:m2}
\end{equation}
The implementation uses bounded/Tobit-style fits for the observed day-window endpoints, with Heckman selection variants available. Prediction of daily activity--travel duration by regression is established practice \citep{Zong2016PredictionRegression}, and the joint discrete--continuous treatment of start/end time with the rest of the schedule follows the commuting-timing formulations of \citet{NurulHabib2009AnModel}. Stages 2 and 3 run \emph{independently} by default; joint Weibull/lognormal estimation with a budget-consistency penalty is opt-in.

\subsection{Stage 3 --- Activity duration}
Lognormal regressions per purpose with post-hoc budget rescaling to $B$:
\begin{equation}
\ln T_{ip}=V_{ip}+\sigma\varepsilon_{ip},\qquad
\varepsilon_{ip}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1),\qquad
\textstyle\sum_{p}T_{ip}\le B_i,
\label{eq:m3}
\end{equation}
The multiple discrete--continuous extreme value (MDCEV) episode allocator \citep{Palma2021MDCEV} (moment or maximum-likelihood estimation) replaces the lognormal by a Kuhn--Tucker allocation of the budget over activity episodes, with the multiple discrete--continuous nested extreme value variant \citep{Calastri2017MDCNEV} as the social-context extension; both are opt-in, not the default. Discrete-time competing-risk duration forms \citep{Kim2017Duration,Dick1995CompetingDuration,VandenBerg2012ADuration} and episode-level joint engagement--duration--destination models \citep{Khaddar2023Episode} are the natural extensions if duration and destination prove jointly decided.

\subsection{Stage 4 --- Schedule placement}
Departure-period random-regret (RRM) models over six discrete periods (early, AM peak, midday, PM peak, evening, late), per purpose:
\begin{equation}
P_i(p)=\frac{e^{V_{ip}}}{\sum_{p'\in\mathcal{PD}}e^{V_{ip'}}}
\quad\text{or}\quad
R_{ip}=\sum_{p'\in\mathcal{PD}}\ln\!\Bigl(1+e^{V_{ip'}-V_{ip}}\Bigr),\quad
P_i(p)=\frac{e^{-R_{ip}}}{\sum_{p'}e^{-R_{ip'}}},
\label{eq:m4}
\end{equation}
with hybrid RUM--RRM \citep{Golshani2018ActivityModels} and full time-of-day-departure (TDD, 21 joint start/end alternatives) forms as options; the regret form is the behavioural alternative to utility maximisation for timing choice \citep{Swait1987RandomConstraints}, and the joint treatment of departure time with mode choice follows \citet{NurulHabib2009AnModel}. The default RRM chooses the period, after which the exact start minute is drawn from the empirical within-period start-time distribution (with a bounded fallback draw), rather than being estimated as a continuous choice. Timing-pressure covariates (day position, cumulative modeled activity time, time remaining, and predicted duration) plus person attributes enter; selected coefficients may take random parameters (mixed logit) as a searched decision. Telework-driven reshaping of non-work timing and duration \citep{Telework2026Timing} is the substantive reason timing pressure, rather than clock time alone, conditions placement. The TDD alternative is the appropriate joint specification when exact start and end times, rather than only departure periods, are the estimands.

\subsection{Stage 5 --- Joint tour mode--destination and trip chains}
Stage~5 estimates tour mode and destination jointly and assembles full trip chains: a two-level nested logit \citep{Ben-Akiva1985DiscreteDemand} over five modes (drive-alone, shared-ride, transit, walk, bike) with motorised (DA/SR/Transit) versus non-motorised (Walk/Bike) nests supplies mode logsums that enter destination utilities directly, so mode-level crash disutility propagates into spatial demand:
\begin{equation}
P_i(m)=\frac{e^{V_{im}/\mu_{b(m)}}\Bigl(\sum_{m'\in b(m)}e^{V_{im'}/\mu_{b(m)}}\Bigr)^{\mu_{b(m)}-1}}
{\sum_{b'}\Bigl(\sum_{m'\in b'}e^{V_{im'}/\mu_{b'}}\Bigr)^{\mu_{b'}}},
\qquad b(m)\in\{\text{Motor},\text{NonMotor}\},
\label{eq:m5m}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{LS}(n,j)=\ln\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\exp\bigl(V_{nmj}^{(m)}\bigr),
\label{eq:m5m-ls}
\end{equation}
Destination choice is multinomial logit over Manski--Lerman-sampled zone sets with sampling correction for the 371-zone universe \citep{Nerella2004Sampling},
\begin{equation}
P_i(j)=\frac{\exp\!\bigl(V^{(d)}_{ij}+o_{ij}\bigr)}
{\sum_{j'\in C_i}\exp\!\bigl(V^{(d)}_{ij'}+o_{ij'}\bigr)},\qquad
|C_i|=20,\quad C_i\subseteq\mathcal{Z},
\label{eq:m5d}
\end{equation}
with log-determinacy of the nested form for mode choice \citep{Ben-Akiva1985DiscreteDemand} and the choice-set sampling-of-alternatives literature justifying the corrected estimator \citep{Nerella2004Sampling,BliemerRoseHess2008Approx,RoseBliemerHensherCollins2008Designing}. Choice-set formation is itself treated as behavioural rather than computational: constrained/parametrised captivity \citep{SwaitBenAkiva1987PLC,Swait1987RandomConstraints}, latent availability and consideration \citep{Calastri2019Mode,Haque2019ICMNL}, and planning-constrained or learned sets \citep{Auld2011PlanningConstrained,Phan2022NewApproach} are all tested arms rather than fixed assumptions.
\texttt{Mu\_Root} is locked at 1.0 and the nest scales are held fixed by default (freely estimated scales collapsed to bounds and were unidentified on HHTS); a test-gated opt-in frees both scales, keeping them only when interior, significant, and non-degenerate, and reverting to fixed on any failure. Alternative-specific constants and the crash term are force-included in every specification. Estimation is by maximum likelihood with elastic-net penalisation held through convergence, with a differentiability back-end retry on stalled first-order steps (see the software appendix). A parametrised-logit-captivity refinement \citep{SwaitBenAkiva1987PLC} is available as a test-gated post-refinement (see Mathematical programming formulation). Note that a four-level nesting with an intermediate car nest is \emph{not} used: freely estimated nest scales collapsed to their bounds and were unidentified, so the two-level fixed-scale tree above is the implemented specification.

\paragraph{Latent-class arm of the Stage-5 mode model.}
The Stage-5 mode kernel \eqref{eq:m5m} admits a discrete-mixture specialisation: the nested logit is retained per class and only the coefficient vector and the mixture weight vary. Writing the mode utility of \eqref{eq:mode} as $V_{im}(\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(q)})$ --- the same linear predictor, with its own coefficients, the same force-included crash term, and the same structural backbone --- the class-conditional choice probability is
\begin{equation}
  P_q(m\mid n)=\frac{e^{V^{(q)}_{im}/\mu}\Bigl(\sum_{m'\in b}e^{V^{(q)}_{im'}/\mu}\Bigr)^{\mu-1}}
  {\sum_{b'}\Bigl(\sum_{m'\in b'}e^{V^{(q)}_{im'}/\mu}\Bigr)^{\mu}},
  \qquad V^{(q)}_{im}=V_{im}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(q)}\bigr),
  \qquad 2\le K\le 4,
  \label{eq:m5m-lc}
\end{equation}
with the class-membership weights
\begin{equation}
  \pi_q(n)=\frac{\exp\!\bigl(\mathbf{z}_n^{\top}\boldsymbol{\gamma}_q\bigr)}
  {\sum_{q'=1}^{K}\exp\!\bigl(\mathbf{z}_n^{\top}\boldsymbol{\gamma}_{q'}\bigr)},
  \qquad \sum_{q}\pi_q(n)=1,\qquad \pi_q\ge 0.01,
  \label{eq:m5m-lc-mem}
\end{equation}
over the person-type pool of \Cref{tab:variables}; membership is estimated, not imposed, so the class shares are themselves a model output. The observed-preference mixture is
\begin{equation}
  P(m\mid n)=\sum_{q=1}^{K}\pi_q(n)\,P_q(m\mid n),
  \label{eq:lc-prob}
\end{equation}
Latent classing is therefore the discrete-mixture complement to the continuous random-parameter mixing of \eqref{eq:m5m}, in the sense of \citet{Walker2002GeneralizedModel}: both relax a single $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ while keeping the same kernel, and both are admissible families of the same block $5m$ through the family gene $\mathbf{y}_{5m}$ \eqref{eq:c-family} with distribution genes in \Cref{tab:notation}.

\paragraph{Propagation of the latent-class arm through downstream choice.}
The class-conditional logsums enter the destination layer as a mixture rather than as a single value. The class-averaged inclusive value is
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{LS}^{\mathrm{LC}}(n,j)=\ln\sum_{q=1}^{K}\pi_q(n)\,\mathrm{IV}_q(n,j),
  \qquad
  \mathrm{IV}_q(n,j)=\sum_{b'}\Bigl(\sum_{m'\in b'}e^{V^{(q)}_{im'}/\mu}\Bigr)^{\mu},
  \label{eq:m5m-ls-lc}
\end{equation}
which reduces to \eqref{eq:m5m-ls} at $K=1$. Destination choice \eqref{eq:dest} then reads
\begin{equation}
  V^{(d)}_{nj}\supset \beta_{\mathrm{LS}}\cdot\mathrm{LS}^{\mathrm{LC}}(n,j),
  \label{eq:m5m-lc-dest}
\end{equation}
so a class-specific crash coefficient $c^{(q)}_{\mathrm{crash}}$ reaches spatial demand through the mixture even for travellers not assigned to class $q$ --- the destination layer inherits heterogeneity from the mode layer rather than being fitted independently. Because the arm still outputs a tour mode and a single logsum, the feeding operator $\Phi$ \eqref{eq:feeding} is unchanged and the invariant interface \eqref{eq:feeding-invariant} already covers the latent-class case, so no downstream stage is refitted or re-interfaced when $\mathbf{y}_{5m}$ switches to the arm.

\paragraph{Hypothesis test for the arm.}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Hypothesis \arabic*.}, leftmargin=2.5em]
  \item \textbf{H0 (single-class):} all classes share one coefficient vector,
  $\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(q)}=\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(q')}$ for all $q,q'$.
  The mode kernel is \eqref{eq:m5m} and destination choice is linked
  through \eqref{eq:m5m-ls}--\eqref{eq:m5d}.
  \item \textbf{H1 (latent-class):} at least one class-specific vector differs.
  The mode kernel is \eqref{eq:m5m-lc} and destination choice is linked
  through \eqref{eq:m5m-ls-lc}--\eqref{eq:dest}.
\end{enumerate}
The class-structure test is the likelihood-ratio statistic of the mode block,
\begin{equation}
  \small
  \begin{aligned}
    \Lambda_{\mathrm{LC}}
      &=-2\Bigl(\hat\ell_{5m}^{(\mathrm{single})}
        -\hat\ell_{5m}^{(\mathrm{LC})}\Bigr)
        \sim\chi^{2}_{\mathrm{df}_{\mathrm{LC}}},\\
    \mathrm{df}_{\mathrm{LC}}
      &=(K-1)\Bigl(\lvert\boldsymbol{\beta}^{(q)}\rvert
        +\lvert\boldsymbol{\gamma}_q\rvert-1\Bigr).
  \end{aligned}
  \label{eq:lc-lr}
  \end{equation}
one degree-of-freedom block per extra class net of the membership normalisation. Because Stage~5 carries three blocks $b\in\{5m,5d,5b\}$ in $\mathcal{B}$, acceptance of the arm is scored by the master objective rather than by a bespoke criterion: the arm is admitted only if the total BIC \eqref{eq:p2}--\eqref{eq:p2-bic} improves, which is exactly the test-gated rule of \eqref{eq:c-lc-joint} \eqref{eq:c-refine}. The GA search therefore evaluates both kernels and keeps whichever wins, and a rejected arm leaves the specification unchanged.

\paragraph{Estimation, identification, and variance.}
The arm is fitted by expectation--maximisation in which the M-step is a closed-form multinomial logit for both the class-conditional mode probabilities and the membership equation, iterating until the change in log-likelihood falls below $10^{-6}$. Standard errors are read off the observed information matrix at the EM fixed point. Label switching is fixed by ordering classes on descending membership share (equivalently, $\pi_q\ge0.01$ \eqref{eq:c-lc} rules out empty classes); the extra $K-1$ classes are then identified by the class-share floor and by the ordering alone. Class-share and membership diagnostics (entropy, class-conditional coefficient spread, and the effective sample size per class) are reported per segment when the arm is selected.

\begin{equation}
U_{ni}^{(m)} = \beta_T T_{ni} + \beta_C C_{ni} + \beta_S S_{ni}^{(m)}
              + \mathbf{x}_{ni}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\gamma} + \varepsilon_{ni},
\label{eq:mode}
\end{equation}
with $S_{ni}^{(m)}$ the mode-specific OD crash exposure and $\varepsilon_{ni}$ GEV. Destination choice is a flat conditional (MNL-equivalent) choice over the universal set of 371 SA2 zones (370 effective --- the unpopulated Lake Manchester catchment carries no size term). Estimation uses Manski--Lerman importance sampling (20 zones per tour), justified by sampling-of-alternatives analysis \citep{Nerella2004Sampling} and efficient-design practice \citep{BliemerRoseHess2008Approx,RoseBliemerHensherCollins2008Designing}; random-constraint choice-set theory \citep{Swait1987RandomConstraints} motivates the hard availability gates. Structural backbone, always retained: size term, mode logsum, log-distance, distance, agglomeration/competition indices, and crash exposure; the search decides enrichments (attractor types, person$\times$distance interactions, agglomeration form, home-pull gravity). Shadow-price calibration aligns predicted destination marginals on application. A trip-level destination sub-model assigns intermediate-stop destinations subject to detour feasibility against the tour anchors, following detour-factor choice-set logic \citep{Mariante2018Detour}; conditioning destination sets on the already-placed schedule follows the planning-constrained approach \citep{Auld2011PlanningConstrained}.
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
V_{nj}^{(d)} ={}& \beta_{\mathrm{LS}} \cdot \mathrm{LS}(n,j) + \beta_d \log \mathrm{dist}_{nj} + \beta_{d2}\,\mathrm{dist}_{nj}
  + \beta_\mathrm{aggl}\sum_{a\in\mathcal{A}}a_{s,a}\,\mathrm{aggl}_a(j)
  + \beta_\mathrm{comp}\,c_s\,\mathrm{comp}(j)\\
&+ c_{\mathrm{crash}}\,\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash}}(n,j) + c_{\mathrm{crash\_safety}}\,\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash\_safety}}(n,j)
  + \log S_j + \mathbf{z}_j^\top \boldsymbol{\gamma},
\end{split}
\label{eq:dest}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{aggl}_a,\mathrm{comp}$ are the agglomeration--competition (ACDC) identities \eqref{eq:acdc} selected by \eqref{eq:c-aggl}--\eqref{eq:c-comp}, $\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash}},\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash\_safety}}$ are the orthogonalised destination crash terms \eqref{eq:ortho-dest} (crash net of log-distance), $\log S_j$ is the destination size term, $\mathrm{LS}(n,j)$ the tour-mode logsum linkage, and $\mathbf{z}_j$ residual attractors. The trip-level (5b) utility has the same form with detour-feasible $C_i$ and anchor caps \eqref{eq:c-detour}.

\subsection{Stage 6 --- Plan validation and finalisation (predict-only)}
No estimation: Stage-5 outputs are validated and repaired (schedule gaps, overnight trips, non-positive travel times, invalid zones), enriched with skim travel times and zone coordinates, and exported DynusT/MATSim-ready. The repair operator $\mathcal{R}$ maps a raw simulated plan to a feasible one by closing the feasibility test of \eqref{eq:c-anchor}--\eqref{eq:c-time} on every tour and leg,
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathbf{y}}'=\mathcal{R}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}),\qquad
\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\bigl\{(o_{t},d_{t},m_{t},t^{o}_{t},t^{d}_{t})\bigr\}_{t\in\mathcal{U}_n},
\label{eq:m6}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\tau(o_{t,l},d_{t,l},m_{t,l})>0,\qquad
t^{o}_{t,l+1}\ge t^{d}_{t,l}+\tau_{t,l},
\label{eq:m6b}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
o_{t,1}=o_t,\quad d_{t,|\mathcal{L}_t|}=d_t,\quad d_{t,l}=o_{t,l+1},\qquad
\sum_{p}T_{np}+\sum_{t,l}\tau_{t,l}\le B_n,\qquad
[t^{o}_{t},t^{d}_{t}]\subseteq[\mathrm{dayStart}_n,\mathrm{dayEnd}_n],
\label{eq:m6c}
\end{equation}
so a plan is exported only when every anchor, continuity, budget, and window condition holds \citep{Gibbons2025EvaluatingModel}. A standalone Stage-6 mode model exists in the codebase as legacy and is not run --- mode choice lives inside Stage~5, and re-running it would overwrite the correctly-estimated joint modes.

