\subsection{Evolutionary feature selection}
The search engine executing (P2) is SparseEA-AGDS (Sparse Evolutionary Algorithm with Adaptive Genetic operators and Dynamic gene Scoring): an evolutionary wrapper whose chromosome is one candidate specification per stage --- the concatenated genes $(\mathbf{g}_s,\mathbf{r}_s,\mathbf{y}_b,K,\boldsymbol{\psi},\mathbf{a}_s,c_s)$ --- and whose fitness is the per-observation mean total BIC of \eqref{eq:p2}, $\overline{-\mathrm{BIC}/n}$ averaged over \emph{all} valid segments of a stage (never a best-segment score), with the significance penalties $\mathrm{SigPen}_s$ \eqref{eq:sigpen} subtracted. The search is estimation-only: every fitness evaluation fits the candidate model on the HHTS estimation frame and prices it; prediction over the synthetic population runs once, after the search, from the selected specifications. The algorithm:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Step \arabic*.}, leftmargin=2.5em]
  \item \textbf{Initialise.} Seed the population with feasible specifications (uniform over searched genes; forced genes $\mathcal{I}_s$/$\mathcal{E}_s$ \eqref{eq:c-force} and the structural backbone pinned; multiple initial solutions, five by default for the crash SA search of \Cref{tab:sa_config}).
  \item \textbf{Evaluate + cache.} For each candidate: repair (at-most-one per collinearity group \eqref{eq:c-coll}, behavioural minima \eqref{eq:c-min}, cardinality bounds \eqref{eq:c-card}), enforce or repair significance, then run the inner penalised MLE per segment. Chromosome-level caching removes duplicate re-estimation; a stalled first-order fit retries once through the differentiability back-end before returning failure fitness.
  \item \textbf{Tier the population.} Rank candidates into fitness tiers; assign per-individual crossover/mutation probabilities from tier rank (not globally), so strong candidates are exposed less to destructive mutation.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic gene scoring (AGDS).} Score every gene position by its marginal contribution across recent evaluations; draw mutation targets by tournament on gene scores rather than i.i.d.\ across positions, concentrating moves on genes whose evidence is genuinely mixed.
  \item \textbf{Move within the neighbourhood.} Accept perturbations only inside $\mathcal{N}_\Delta(\mathbf{g}_s^0)$ \eqref{eq:c-nhood} (at most $\Delta_s$ gene flips); functional-form and set moves ($\mathbf{y}_b$: PLC/M3/ML/LC arms) additionally require $\Delta\mathrm{BIC}< -2$ \emph{and} improved validation \eqref{eq:c-refine}, so a rejected arm never changes the reported specification. Random-parameter genes toggle through a greedy Halton-draw pass (draw-count toggles tried as local polish).
  \item \textbf{Adapt and re-escape.} Adaptive mutation floors respond to population diversity (mean Hamming distance); stagnation reheats the temperature rate when the best-so-far fitness stalls; multi-restart mini-runs compete against the main lineage.
  \item \textbf{Terminate and publish.} Stop on budget or convergence of best-so-far fitness; the champion is re-fit full-sample, staged through $\Phi$, calibrated by $\mathcal{K}$, and archived with per-segment trajectories, coefficient tables, and the selected gene vector (see the software appendix for the run-tree layout).
\end{enumerate}

\noindent Hyperparameters of the crash-pair SA searches are listed in \Cref{tab:sa_config} (five initial solutions, five steps per temperature, cooling $\alpha=0.975$, initial acceptance 0.5, iteration and wall-clock caps); the production hyperparameters of the behavioural-stage search (population size, $\Delta_s$ move budget, mutation floors, stagnation threshold, elastic-net penalty weights) are named quantities of the run manifest, fixed per run tag rather than tuned per stage, and their values ship with every archived run tree. All numerical exhibits in Experiments derive from the archived runs whose manifest values are thereby auditable.

\subsection{Post-selection inference: what the search costs, and how we pay for it}
\label{sec:postselect}

The specification search of \eqref{eq:p2} is a data-adaptive selection device: it prices thousands of candidate specifications on the same frame $\mathcal{D}_{s,k}$ and returns the winner. Every quantity the paper subsequently reports \emph{about that winner} --- coefficients, standard errors, $t$-ratios, $p$-values, and likelihood-ratio tests against it --- is a post-selection quantity whose naive sampling distribution is not the textbook one, because the same observations both chose the specification and are asked to certify it. Three distortions bite here. \emph{(i)~Optimism in fit}: the reported BIC is the minimum over the search path, so it is biased downward by an amount that grows with the number of evaluated specifications --- which is why $\Delta\mathrm{BIC}$ against a baseline is reported but never used on its own as evidence of a genuine effect. \emph{(ii)~Selection bias in signs and magnitudes}: among weak covariates, the terms a search keeps are disproportionately those whose sample noise happened to be favourable, so searched coefficients are biased away from zero and their signs against the prior. \emph{(iii)~Invalid nominal $p$-values}: the $\chi^2$ and normal reference distributions behind \eqref{eq:c-sig} and \eqref{eq:lc-lr} condition on a specification fixed \emph{ex ante}, which no searched specification is.

Two classes of quantity in this paper are exempt, and they are the ones carrying the safety claims. First, the safety terms are force-included in $\mathcal{I}_s$ \eqref{eq:c-force} in every specification and every search restart, so the crash coefficients are estimated in a specification that no search chose and their likelihood-ratio tests have nominal coverage. Second, the between-arm contrast
\begin{equation}
\Delta\mathrm{Err}=\mathrm{Err}(P0)-\mathrm{Err}(P2)
\label{eq:deltaerr}
\end{equation}
is a comparison of two \emph{fixed} evaluation procedures on a frame that played no part in either selection, so it needs no distributional argument at all. Everything else is corrected by the following procedures, whose results are reported in Experiments.

\paragraph{(PS-I) Sample splitting.}
Split $\mathcal{D}_{s,k}$ into $\mathcal{D}^{(1)}_{s,k}$ and $\mathcal{D}^{(2)}_{s,k}$ by person, stratified on segment, with a fixed seed. The search runs on $\mathcal{D}^{(1)}$ only; the selected specification is then estimated on $\mathcal{D}^{(2)}$, and \emph{only} the $\mathcal{D}^{(2)}$ fit is used for coefficients, standard errors, $t$-ratios, and $p$-values:
\begin{equation}
(\hat{\mathbf{g}}_s,\hat{\mathbf{y}}_b)=\arg\min_{\mathbf{g}_s,\mathbf{y}_b}
\mathrm{BIC}_{s,k}\bigl(\mathcal{D}^{(1)}_{s,k};\mathbf{g}_s,\mathbf{y}_b\bigr),\qquad
\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{(2)}_{s,k}=\arg\max_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}
\ell\bigl(\mathcal{D}^{(2)}_{s,k};\hat{\mathbf{g}}_s,\hat{\mathbf{y}}_b\bigr),
\label{eq:split}
\end{equation}
Because $\mathcal{D}^{(2)}$ is statistically independent of the selection event, the ordinary reference distributions are valid on it. The price is precision, so full-sample fits are reported alongside for comparison.

\paragraph{(PS-II) Post-selection (conditional) inference.}
Conditioning on the selection event truncates the reference law of each searched statistic to the acceptance region that selection implies. For a term $j$ with selection region $[\mathcal{L}_j,\mathcal{U}_j]$ induced by the active constraints on $\hat{\mathbf{g}}_s\in\mathcal{N}_\Delta(\mathbf{g}_s^0)$ \eqref{eq:nhood}, the post-selection tail probability is
\begin{equation}
\Pr\bigl(\hat t_j\le x\mid \hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{\backslash j}, \hat t_j\in[\mathcal{L}_j,\mathcal{U}_j]\bigr)
=\frac{F_{j}\bigl(x;\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{\backslash j}\bigr)-F_{j}\bigl(\mathcal{L}_j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{\backslash j}\bigr)}
{F_{j}\bigl(\mathcal{U}_j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{\backslash j}\bigr)-F_{j}\bigl(\mathcal{L}_j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{\backslash j}\bigr)},\qquad x\in[\mathcal{L}_j,\mathcal{U}_j],
\label{eq:selinf}
\end{equation}
with $F_j$ the usual normal law; the naive $p$-value is the same expression with the truncation removed, so the ratio of the two is the correction the search demands. Where the selection region is not polyhedral in closed form, the conditional distribution is obtained by the truncated rejection sampling described with the bootstrap below.

\paragraph{(PS-III) Search-aware parametric bootstrap.}
The correction that prices the search machinery itself: simulate frames from the published fit, then re-run the \emph{entire} search on each simulated frame with the same budget, constraints, and seeds,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}^{(b)}\sim P_{\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{sel}},\qquad
\bigl(\hat{\mathbf{g}}^{(b)},\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(b)},\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{(b)}\bigr)
=\mathrm{Search}\bigl(\mathcal{D}^{(b)}\bigr),\qquad b=1,\dots,B,
\label{eq:psboot}
\end{equation}
so the bootstrap distribution of any statistic already contains the optimism the search injects. Two summaries are read off it. The \emph{selection frequency}
\begin{equation}
\hat q_j=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\mathbf{1}\bigl\{g^{(b)}_{s,j}=1\bigr\},
\label{eq:selfreq}
\end{equation}
is the fraction of searches that retain term $j$; a term with $\hat q_j$ below one half is a term the search does not reliably find, whatever its in-sample $t$-ratio, and it is reported as a held prior rather than an effect. The \emph{search-inflated critical value} is the empirical quantile of $\{|\hat t^{(b)}_j|\}$ against the nominal $z_{1-\alpha/2}$, giving the factor by which naive thresholds must be widened.

\paragraph{(PS-IV) Stability under sample perturbation.}
For subsample fractions $\rho\in\{0.5,0.7,0.9\}$, the search is repeated on person-level subsamples of the selection frame; the reported diagnostics are the Jaccard overlap of the selected gene vectors against the published specification and the coefficient dispersion across subsamples. A specification whose membership changes when a tenth of the data changes is not an identified specification, and such a specification is reported with its perturbation panel rather than a single coefficient table.

\paragraph{Reporting rule.}
Each searched quantity is reported three times: naive, PS-I (split-sample), and PS-II/PS-III corrected. Force-included terms are reported once, with their nominal coverage, and flagged \emph{forced}. The significance constraint \eqref{eq:c-sig} is enforced \emph{inside} the search as a repair, never re-interpreted outside it as a statement about the population.
Each stage searches binary feature masks $\mathbf{g}\in\{0,1\}^m$ and functional-form selectors $\mathbf{y}$ minimising the sum-total BIC over all stages and segments subject to significance, perturbation-neighbourhood, and declarative specification constraints --- BIC as primary criterion rather than coefficient $p$-values \citep{Hess2026Significance}, with coefficient precision entering as explicit significance constraints on searched inclusions (plus a significance penalty over the weak-parameter fraction and a held-out Jensen--Shannon penalty on destination shares as the engine's exact-penalty implementation, formalised in the mathematical program below) --- subject to a declarative constraint architecture: forced inclusions/exclusions, at-most-one-per-collinearity-group repair, behavioural minima, and random-parameter eligibility (never/forced/free, with a greedy Halton-draw toggle pass; heterogeneity motivation \citep{AllenbyRossi1998Heterogeneity,HessRose2009Intra}). The search engine throughout is SparseEA-AGDS: per-individual crossover/mutation probabilities scaled by fitness-tier rank, with per-generation dynamic gene scoring (mutation targets chosen by tournament on gene scores rather than i.i.d.), layered over fitness caching, adaptive-mutation floors, stagnation reheat, and collinearity repair. Estimator-specific inner fits use count likelihoods for stages~1--3, regret likelihoods for stage~4, and GEV likelihoods for stage~5, with an opt-in Metropolis sampled-choice refinement of the Stage-5 destination fit (see Mathematical programming formulation). All numerical exhibits in Experiments (\Cref{tab:modelfit,tab:destval,tab:modeval}, Figures~2--3) are interim estimates from the predecessor search configuration and will be refreshed from the SparseEA-AGDS re-run; the model forms, constraint architecture, and validation protocol described here are those of the re-run. All maximum-likelihood fits share an elastic-net penalty through convergence (removed before inference), and mixed-logit refinement falls back to the nested logit on non-movement. Segments with fewer than 50 observations are excluded from fitness.

\subsection{Mathematical programming formulation}
\begingroup\small\allowdisplaybreaks
The paper's comparison is scoped below as three programs: (P0) the pure-ActivitySim baseline --- published specifications, fitted normally, universal choice sets; (P1) the crash-prediction model search; and (P2) the full activity-based specification search as a bilevel mixed-integer program --- minimising sum-total BIC over all stages and segments subject to significance, perturbation-neighbourhood, and specification constraints, with functional-form selection and tested set refinements. (P0) and (P2) share estimators, the feeding operator $\Phi$, and the calibration operator $\mathcal{K}$ defined below: the \emph{only} differences are specification authority (fixed ActivitySim vs.\ searched) and choice-set treatment (universal vs.\ tested refinements). The error gap between the arms is therefore attributable to modelling decisions, not machinery.

\paragraph{(P1) Crash-frequency specification search.}
Index road stereotypes $s\in\mathcal{S}$ ($|\mathcal{S}|=6$) and crash types
$t\in\mathcal{T}$ ($|\mathcal{T}|=5$); 28 of the 30 pairs are estimable.
For each pair $(s,t)$, choose a count-model specification $m_{st}$ from
$\{\text{Poisson}, \text{NB}, \text{random-parameter Poisson},
\text{random-parameter NB}\}\times\text{covariate transforms}\times
\text{mixing distributions}$ to
\begin{equation}
\min_{m_{st}} \quad \mathrm{BIC}(m_{st}) = -2\,\hat\ell_{st} + k_{st}\ln n_{st},
\label{eq:p1}
\end{equation}
solved by simulated annealing (780--5,011 specifications evaluated per pair;
restricted non-random search where $>99\%$ zero inflation blocks random-parameter
convergence). Fitted functions predict segment frequencies for 2020--2023, blended
with observed counts by Empirical Bayes weights from the fitted overdispersion,
and accumulated along inter-zonal shortest paths into the OD crash matrix.

\paragraph{(P0) Pure-ActivitySim baseline (fit normally).}
Index the same stages and segments as (P2). Per stage $s$, fix the published
ActivitySim specification \citep{Board2014Activity-BasedPrimer} $\bar{\mathbf{g}}_s$ with no search loop: CDAP
mandatory/non-mandatory/home MNL on person, household, and accessibility
terms; tour mode choice on IVT$\times$purpose, age$\times$mode, household
size$\times$SR, vehicle availability, licence, and gender (trip mode choice
adds position-in-tour flags); location choice on size, distance, and logsum
terms; durations and scheduling on the standard conditional forms. Choice
sets are universal --- all 371 in-scope zones (370 populated), all five modes --- subject \emph{only}
to ActivitySim hard availability (DA requires licence and household vehicle;
walk/bike require distance thresholds; transit requires finite skim impedance
at prediction), shared
identically with (P2). Parameters solve plain maximum likelihood,
\begin{equation}
\max_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_s} \sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_s} \hat\ell_{s,k}
(\boldsymbol{\theta}_s; \bar{\mathbf{g}}_s),
\label{eq:p0}
\end{equation}
with no selection penalties and no set refinements (no captivity, no
schedule conditioning, no consideration stage). The (P0) family is fixed at
the default form per stage ($\mathbf{y}_s$ at the configuration default), so
(P0) vs.\ (P2) differs in specification authority \emph{and} functional-form
freedom. Operationally this is the
same fitters, feeding, and calibration as (P2) with fixed feature overrides
and the search loop disabled.

\paragraph{Feeding operator $\Phi$ (shared by both arms): how stages link.}
Let $\mathbf{x}_s$ be the population state entering stage $s$ (person/tour
records plus skim matrices $\mathbf{Z}$) and $\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_s$
the fitted parameters. The next state is
$\mathbf{x}_{s+1}=\Phi_s(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_s,\mathbf{x}_s;\mathbf{Z})$.
$\Phi$ is identical in (P0) and (P2); only the specifications differ. Stage by stage:
\begin{equation}
\small
\begin{aligned}
\text{S0}\!\to\!\text{S1:}&\quad d_n,\mathbf{d}_{h(n)}\ \text{(CDAP pattern + household coordination) condition activity counts } y_{np};\\
\text{S1}\!\to\!\text{S2:}&\quad \{y_{np}\}_p,\ \text{tour count }|\mathcal{U}_n|\ \text{condition day budget } B_n\ \text{and }[\mathrm{dayStart}_n,\mathrm{dayEnd}_n];\\
\text{S2}\!\to\!\text{S3:}&\quad B_n\ \text{rescales durations: } \sum_p T_{np}\le B_n\ \text{(post-hoc rescaling to $B_n$);} \\
\text{S1--S3}\!\to\!\text{S4:}&\quad (y_{np},B_n,T_{np})\ \text{form timing pressure (day position, consumed/remaining time, predicted duration) placing }(t^o_t,t^d_t);\\
\text{S4}\!\to\!\text{S5/5b:}&\quad \text{placed windows bound destination feasibility and 5b detour caps \eqref{eq:c-time}--\eqref{eq:c-detour};}\\
\text{S5m}\!\to\!\text{S5d:}&\quad \text{tour-mode logsum carries mode (incl.\ crash) disutility into destination choice:}\\
&\quad \mathrm{LS}(n,j)=\ln\!\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\exp\!\bigl(V_{nmj}^{(m)}/\mu\bigr)^{\!\mu}\ \text{(nest form \eqref{eq:m5m}; class-averaged \eqref{eq:m5m-lc} if LC),}\\
&\quad V^{(d)}_{nj}\ \text{includes } \beta_{\mathrm{LS}}\!\cdot\!\mathrm{LS}(n,j)\ \text{by \eqref{eq:dest};}\\
\text{P1}\!\to\!\text{S5 + upstream:}&\quad \text{EB crash skim $\mathrm{CRASH}_{jk}$ \eqref{eq:crash-od} enters mode $S^{(m)}_{ni}$ and destination $\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash}}$ \eqref{eq:ortho-dest},}\\
&\quad \text{plus home-zone crash accessibility $\log(1+\sum_j e^{-\beta t_{ij}}\mathrm{CRASH}_{ij})$ offered to S0--S1;}\\
\text{S5}\!\to\!\text{S6:}&\quad \text{tours+legs must satisfy \eqref{eq:c-anchor}--\eqref{eq:c-detour} (repaired in Stage~6).}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:feeding}
\end{equation}
Every stage is estimated on HHTS ($\mathbf{x}_0$) and the fitted chain applied to the synthetic population. The logsum is the key cross-stage price: a mode-level attribute (time, cost, crash) reshapes spatial demand without re-estimating destination preferences.
\paragraph{Feeding holds for every family variant (variant-invariant interface).} Stages may use different models ($y_b$ picks $f\in\mathcal{F}_b$), but $\Phi_s$ is defined for \emph{all} $f$ with a common output state, so a family switch never breaks the downstream feed. Formally, $\forall b\in\mathcal{B},\forall f\in\mathcal{F}_b:\ \Phi_s^{(f)}:\mathcal{X}_s\to\mathcal{X}_{s+1}$ with fixed $\mathcal{X}_{s+1}$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{S0: }&\{MNL,MXL\}\!+\!\text{joint}\to d_n\!\in\!\{H,N,M\},\mathbf{d}_h &&\text{(MXL integrates out mixing; joint draws household-coherent $d_n$);}\\
\text{S1: }&\{Poisson,NB,\text{ord.\ logit}\}\to y_{np}\!\in\!\mathbb{Z}_{\ge0} &&\text{(all output counts; guardrails \eqref{eq:c-count} apply regardless);}\\
\text{S2: }&\{OLS{+}logn,Tobit,Heckman,joint\}\to (B_n,\mathrm{dayStart}_n,\mathrm{dayEnd}_n) &&\text{(levels in minutes);}\\
\text{S3: }&\{lognormal,MDCEV\}\to T_{np}>0,\ \sum_pT_{np}\!\le\! B_n &&\text{(MDCEV Kuhn--Tucker allocation rescaled to $B_n$ like lognormal);}\\
\text{S4: }&\{RRM,\text{hybrid},TDD\}\!\times\!\{MNL,MXL\}\to (t^o_t,t^d_t)\!\in\!\mathcal{PD} &&\text{(TDD 21 start/end cells projected to 6 periods; MXL integrates out);}\\
\text{S5m: }&\begin{aligned}[t]\{NL,NL_{\mu},MNL,MXL,GSE\}\!\times\!\{\text{single},LC\}\!\times\!PLC\\
\to\ &(m_t,\mathrm{LS}(n,j))\end{aligned}
&&\text{(all output a tour mode + logsum in \emph{same} utils);}\\
\text{S5d: }&\{univ,M3,ML\}\to j_t\!\in\!\mathcal{Z} &&\text{(M3/ML only shrink $C_i$; $o_{ij}$ correction keeps scale comparable);}\\
\text{S5b: }&\{detour\text{-}MNL\}\to j_{t,l}\ \text{detour-feasible \eqref{eq:c-detour}.}&&
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:feeding-invariant}
\end{equation}
Hence \eqref{eq:feeding} holds $\forall\mathbf{y}_b$ satisfying \eqref{eq:c-family}: the MIP searches families, $\Phi$ transports only the invariant outputs above, and Stage-6 validation \eqref{eq:c-anchor}--\eqref{eq:c-detour} plus $\mathcal{K}$ apply identically. (P0) is the special case $\mathbf{y}_b=$ default with the same $\Phi$. The fine print of the S5m row: every admissible kernel outputs the tour mode and a logsum expressed in the same utilities --- NL/MNL in closed form \eqref{eq:m5m}; MXL/GSE/LC class- or mixture-averaged \eqref{eq:m5m-lc}; PLC only re-weights shares --- so no downstream stage depends on which kernel was picked.

\paragraph{Calibration operator $\mathcal{K}$ (shared by both arms).}
Post-estimation, likelihood-invariant aggregate matching. Mode ASCs iterate
$\mathrm{ASC}_m \leftarrow \mathrm{ASC}_m+\log(\mathrm{obs}_m/\mathrm{pred}_m)$
on SR/Walk/Bike/Transit against the DA reference (at most 8 rounds,
converged when all adjustments fall below 0.002); destination marginals are
matched by shadow-price iteration (50 rounds, step 0.25, clip 5.0) with
origin-specific OD calibration biases (step 0.6). $\mathcal{K}$ changes no
likelihood and is applied identically in both arms, so any error gap is
specification, not calibration effort.

\paragraph{(P2) Framework summary: sets, inputs, decisions.}
\Cref{tab:notation} collates the program primitives. The candidate model space per block is the family--feature product
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_b=\{(f,G):\ f\in\mathcal{F}_b,\ G\subseteq V_s\},
\label{eq:modelspace}
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal{F}_b$ in \eqref{eq:families} (what model forms are considered; $\mathcal{F}_{5m}$ is kernel $\times$ LC-mixing \eqref{eq:m5m-lc} $\times$ captivity) and $V_s$ the stage candidate pool in \Cref{tab:variables} (what variables are considered: person attributes $\mathbf{z}_n$, household attributes $\mathbf{z}^H_h$, size $S_j$, skims); search constraints \eqref{eq:c-family}--\eqref{eq:c-refine} carve the admissible specification subset, while schedule-consistency constraints \eqref{eq:c-anchor}--\eqref{eq:c-detour} carve the admissible simulated plans (anchors-in-trips, mode dominance, counts, time, detour). Feeding \eqref{eq:feeding} holds for every family variant via the invariant interface \eqref{eq:feeding-invariant}. The outer search picks exactly one $(f,G)\in\mathcal{M}_b$ per block via $(\mathbf{g}_s,\mathbf{r}_s,\mathbf{y}_b)$; the inner fit prices each pick by penalised MLE.
