\paragraph{(P2) Schedule-consistency constraints: what ties tours to trips.} \eqref{eq:c-anchor}--\eqref{eq:c-detour} are not part of the BIC search; they are feasibility rules the simulator must satisfy when it converts sampled choices into full-day plans, enforced in the feeding operator $\Phi$ (Stage-4 placed times bound Stage-5 windows and 5b detour feasibility) and repaired in Stage~6 validation. Concretely: (i)~\textbf{Anchors in trips \eqref{eq:c-anchor}:} every tour $t$ of person $n$ has anchors $(o_t,d_t)$ (usually home at both ends; work-anchored for work-based sub-tours). Its legs must chain exactly: the first leg starts at $o_t$, the last leg ends at $d_t$, and each leg's destination is the next leg's origin ($d_{t,l}=o_{t,l+1}$). A tour anchor can never be skipped by its trips. (ii)~\textbf{Dominant-mode consistency \eqref{eq:c-modedom}:} each leg has a trip mode $m_{t,l}$ but the tour carries a single main mode $m_t$ given by the main-mode rule $f^{\mathrm{main}}$ (hierarchy transit $>$ DA $>$ SR $>$ bike $>$ walk; walk-suffixed legs read as transit access per ActivitySim convention). The tour mode is therefore the dominant trip mode by construction, and incompatible leg modes (e.g.\ auto-tour legs switching to walk mid-tour) are rejected/repaired. (iii)~\textbf{Activity-count conservation \eqref{eq:c-count}:} Stage~1 counts $y_{np}$ must equal what the schedule actually generates: each tour contributes one primary activity plus $(|\mathcal{L}_t|-1)$ intermediate stops, summed over $\mathcal{U}_n$. Mandatory guardrails (full-time workers / school children / full-time tertiary students cannot draw zero mandatory activities) are hard floors. (iv)~\textbf{Time feasibility \eqref{eq:c-time}:} durations plus leg travel times $\tau(\cdot)$ cannot exceed the day budget $B_n$ (post-hoc rescaling to $B_n$ in Stage~3); legs must be ordered and non-overlapping ($t^o_{t,l+1}\ge t^d_{t,l}+\tau_{t,l}$); every placed window must sit inside $[\mathrm{dayStart}_n,\mathrm{dayEnd}_n]$ from Stage~2. (v)~\textbf{Detour feasibility \eqref{eq:c-detour}:} Stage-5b intermediate-stop destinations $j_{t,l}$ are only admissible if the detour off the tour anchors $(o_t,d_t)$ respects maximum-detour, detour-ratio, and base-distance caps. Violations are repaired or rejected in Stage~6 (schedule gaps, overnight trips, non-positive travel times, invalid zones), so every exported DynusT/MATSim plan satisfies \eqref{eq:c-anchor}--\eqref{eq:c-detour} by construction in both (P0) and (P2).
The perturbation neighbourhood around the incumbent $\mathbf{g}_s^0$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{N}_\Delta(\mathbf{g}^0)=\{\mathbf{g}\in\{0,1\}^{m_s}:\ \|\mathbf{g}-\mathbf{g}^0\|_1\le\Delta_s,\ \text{\eqref{eq:c-force}--\eqref{eq:c-card} hold}\},
\label{eq:nhood}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ each accepted perturbation flips at most $\Delta_s$ genes (directed, bandit, and tournament moves; distribution-gene moves restricted to eligible level-of-service terms; Halton-draw toggle as local polish; stagnation reheat as the escape). Functional-form and set moves ($\mathbf{y}_b$: PLC/M3/ML arms) additionally require $\Delta\mathrm{BIC}<-2$ \emph{and} improved validation, so rejected arms leave the specification unchanged and every reported refinement demonstrably reduces error against (P0). Significance \eqref{eq:c-sig} couples the search to coefficient precision: every \emph{searched} inclusion must clear $p\le\alpha$ (equivalently $|t|\ge z_{1-\alpha/2}$) in each segment where it is estimated. Forced inclusions $\mathcal{I}_s$ (crash term in mode choice, alternative-specific constants, structural backbone) are exempt by design --- structural, not searched --- and are validated instead by likelihood-ratio tests (e.g.\ crash LR: work $p<0.05$; education/non-mandatory inconclusive), which is why BIC-based \emph{selection} evidence and coefficient-level \emph{significance} are reported as distinct dimensions throughout Experiments. The engine prices \eqref{eq:c-sig} violations through exact penalty plus repair: with $\mathcal{P}_s$ the free parameters of stage $s$ and $D_s$ the degeneracy event ($>80\%$ of free mode parameters weak),
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SigPen}_s = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}_s|}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{P}_s}\mathbf{1}\{j\text{ weak}\} + M\cdot\mathbf{1}\{D_s\},
\label{eq:sigpen}
\end{equation}
where weak means stuck, at-bound, unidentified, near-zero, or insignificant, and $M\gg 1$ is the flat-degeneracy sentinel weight; the fitness subtracts $\mathrm{SigPen}_s$. MAE proper is reported in validation (destination-share MAE), with held-out JSD playing the aggregate-fidelity role inside fitness. All maximum-likelihood fits share the elastic-net penalty through convergence (removed before inference); mixed-logit refinement falls back to the nested logit on non-movement; segments with fewer than 50 observations are excluded from fitness.

\paragraph{(P2) destination-layer data and ACDC as MIP identities + orthogonality constraints.}
The destination search in (P2) estimates on the ACDC case--alternative frame $\mathcal{D}_{s,k}$: tours $i$ (cases) crossed with Manski--Lerman-sampled alternatives $j$ (20 zones), carrying OD skims (distance, time, crash exposure), zone attractors (size terms, land use, accessibility, ACDC below), mode logsums, person$\times$zone interactions, home-pull gravity, and the sampling log-correction. Agglomeration and competition are \emph{deterministic identities} (precomputed columns, not estimated) over the segment size attractor $S_k$ and auto time $t_{jk}$, promoted to full MIP decisions via \eqref{eq:c-aggl}--\eqref{eq:c-comp}:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{aggl}_a[j]
  &=\log\!\Bigl(\sum_{k\ne j}S^{(a)}_k
    e^{-\kappa t_{jk}}+\varepsilon\Bigr),\\
\mathrm{comp}[j]
  &=\sum_{k\ne j}e^{-\kappa t_{jk}}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:acdc}
\end{equation}
with spatial-decay rate $\kappa>0$ and $\varepsilon>0$ a small constant
avoiding $\log 0$ (both fixed inputs, not searched). Here
$S^{(a)}_k$ is the theme-$a$ size slice (retail, office, education, leisure,
parks, health, food, or civic), and $S_k$ is the pooled size for the generic
form. They enter the destination utility as priced attributes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
V_{ij}={}&\cdots+
\beta_{\mathrm{aggl}}\sum_{a\in\mathcal{A}}a_{s,a}\,\mathrm{aggl}_a[j]\\
&+\beta_{\mathrm{comp}}c_s\,\mathrm{comp}[j]+\cdots .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:acdc-mip}
\end{equation}
so $(\mathbf{a}_s,c_s)$ select \emph{which} columns the inner MLE can price: \eqref{eq:c-aggl} forces exactly one themed $\mathrm{aggl}_a$ active per spec (at-most-one mutex plus require-one; the generic pooled form is the $a_0$ fallback), while \eqref{eq:c-comp} switches competition on/off ($c_s=0\implies\beta_{\mathrm{comp},s}=0$; $c_s=1\implies\beta_{\mathrm{comp}}$ freely estimated subject to \eqref{eq:c-sig}--\eqref{eq:c-sign}). Both coefficients are inner variables in $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s,k}$, penalised in $\mathrm{BIC}_{s,k}$ like any searched term.
\paragraph{(P2) Orthogonality as MIP equalities --- destination in full.} Orthogonalisation is deterministic preprocessing promoted to MIP equalities \eqref{eq:c-ortho-dest}--\eqref{eq:c-ortho-mode}: for each declared pair $(c,b)$ whose expressions resolve to plain dataset columns $x_c,x_b$ jointly present in the frame,
\begin{equation}
\tilde{x}_c = x_c - \beta_{cb}\,x_b,\qquad
\beta_{cb}=\frac{\mathrm{Cov}(x_c,x_b)}{\mathrm{Var}(x_b)},
\label{eq:ortho}
\end{equation}
pooled OLS on the finite mask ($n>10$, $\mathrm{Var}>10^{-12}$, $|\beta|\le10^6$), so $\mathrm{Cov}(\tilde{x}_c,x_b)=0$ by the OLS normal equations and $\tilde{x}_c$ identifies the net effect. Materialisation precedes Dataset construction (verified swap: the utility references $\tilde{x}_c$ only if the column exists in dataset variables, else keeps $x_c$ with a warning). The destination block uses its own pair set $\mathcal{O}^{\mathrm{dest}}$:
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:ortho-dest-block}
\begin{align}
\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash}}(n,j) &= c_{\mathrm{crash}}(n,j)-\beta_{\mathrm{crash}\cdot\log d}\,c_{\mathrm{log\_dist}}(n,j), && \mathrm{Cov}(\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash}},c_{\mathrm{log\_dist}})=0 \label{eq:ortho-dest}\\
\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash\_safety}}(n,j) &= c_{\mathrm{crash\_safety\_dest}}(n,j)-\beta_{\mathrm{safety}\cdot\log d}\,c_{\mathrm{log\_dist}}(n,j), && \mathrm{Cov}(\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash\_safety}},c_{\mathrm{log\_dist}})=0 \label{eq:ortho-dest2}\\
V^{(d)}_{nj}\ \text{uses}\ &\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash}},\tilde{x}_{\mathrm{crash\_safety}}\ \text{in place of raw crash,} && c_{\mathrm{crash}}\le 0\ \text{(sign bound)} \label{eq:c-ortho-dest}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
i.e.\ destination crash enters \eqref{eq:dest} only net of trip length, so $c_{\mathrm{crash}}$ is crash sensitivity independent of distance (not a distance proxy). The mode block is symmetric with $\mathcal{O}^{\mathrm{mode}}=\{(\text{time terms}, \mathrm{DIST}),(c_{\mathrm{crash\_veh}},\mathrm{DIST}),(c_{\mathrm{incp}_*},c_{\mathrm{veh}_*})\}$:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{x}^{\mathrm{mode}}_c=x^{\mathrm{mode}}_c-\beta_{cb}x^{\mathrm{mode}}_b,\quad \mathrm{Cov}(\tilde{x}_c,x_b)=0\quad\forall (c,b)\in\mathcal{O}^{\mathrm{mode}}, \label{eq:c-ortho-mode}
\end{equation}
(time/crash net of distance; income net of vehicles). Orthogonalisation never fixes values, only identification; the orthogonalised columns are what \eqref{eq:acdc-mip}--\eqref{eq:dest} price.
\paragraph{(P2) Metropolis sampled-choice estimator (opt-in inner fit).}
The Stage-5 destination inner fit is maximum likelihood by default; an opt-in refinement selects among Metropolis--Hastings, Gibbs, and stochastic-gradient estimators, applied \emph{after} the baseline maximum-likelihood fit on the same case--alternative frame $\mathcal{D}_{s,k}$ above (compact features: log distance, distance, size term, mode logsum, log crash frequency). On success its coefficients overwrite the baseline destination coefficients (log-likelihood recorded alongside); on any failure the baseline fit is kept, so the arm can only replace, never remove, the baseline inner fit. All three methods maximise the McFadden-corrected sampled likelihood over the $K=20$ sampled alternatives --- the 371-zone universal denominator is never formed:
\begin{equation}
\ell(\boldsymbol{\beta})=\sum_{i}\left[V_{ij^*(i)}(\boldsymbol{\beta})+o_{ij^*(i)}-\log\sum_{j\in C_i}\exp(V_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\beta})+o_{ij})\right],
\label{eq:mh-ll}
\end{equation}
with $j^*(i)$ the chosen zone, $C_i$ the sampled set, and $o_{ij}$ the sampling log-correction carried in the frame. \texttt{mh} is random-walk Metropolis (default 1000 draws, 500 burn-in) with adaptive Gaussian proposals $q(\cdot\mid\boldsymbol{\beta})=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\lambda\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t)$ ($\lambda=2.38^2/d$, Haario recursive covariance, block-wise over parameter blocks where applicable) and symmetric acceptance $\min(1,\pi(\boldsymbol{\beta}^*)/\pi(\boldsymbol{\beta}))$ against the Gaussian-prior posterior. \texttt{gibbs} alternates $\boldsymbol{\beta}\mid C$ (inner random-walk steps, default 2) with $C\mid\boldsymbol{\beta}$ (consideration-set refresh from an HHTS Dirichlet-smoothed competing prior blended with current utilities; default 500 draws, 250 burn-in). \texttt{sgd} is adaptive stochastic-gradient MLE (Adam minibatches, $K$ adapted in $[8,64]$ on set effective sample size; default 500 iterations, batch 64). Reported diagnostics are the acceptance rate, bulk effective sample size, and split-$\hat R$. On synthetic 371-zone data (400 cases, 12 sampled alternatives) all three recover true coefficients within 0.35 with sane acceptance (MH $0.31$), and the likelihood never sees more than $K$ alternatives per case (estimation module with synthetic proof included alongside the pipeline).
