\subsection{Crash-frequency workflow and safety experiments}\label{sec:crash-workflow}
The five-step SPF$\rightarrow$EB$\rightarrow$OD-accumulation workflow supplies
the \texttt{CRASH} skim and a home-zone crash-accessibility measure for
upstream layers. Safety enters spatial demand through two pathways. First, the
mode logsum carries mode-level crash disutility into destination choice.
Second, a direct zone crash term penalises high-crash destinations as
neighbourhoods. The feature search determines which pathway is retained.

Upstream, a home-zone crash-accessibility index
$\log(1+\sum_j e^{-\beta t_{ij}}\mathrm{CRASH}_{ij})$ is available to the
activity-participation layer. EB blending reproduces observed segment totals
closely (for example, 1,977 predicted versus 1,977 observed crashes for the
rural-undivided run-off-road category).

Experiments compare safety-integrated ($\mathcal{M}_S$) and baseline
($\mathcal{M}_0$) specifications on identical features. We report
likelihood-ratio tests, $\Delta\rho^2$, crash-coefficient $t$-ratios,
mode-share shifts, destination-distance shifts, and zone-level active-mode
effects. The comparison always evaluates both arms against the same true
\texttt{CRASH} exposure; no crash-reduction or safety-scaling counterfactual
is used.

\paragraph{Step 1: network stratification.} The Queensland network was categorised into six road stereotypes (rural undivided, rural remote, urban undivided, urban divided, urban local, divided freeways/highways) with crashes in five types (run-off-road, head-on, rear-end, intersection, other). Of 30 stereotype--type combinations, 28 are estimable (intersection crashes on divided freeways/highways and head-on crashes on urban local roads lack sufficient data).

\paragraph{Step 2: specification search.} The specification search explores Poisson, negative-binomial, and random-parameter variants with flexible covariate transformations and mixing distributions under simulated annealing, selecting by BIC with hypothesis testing to retain only significant predictors (\Cref{tab:sa_config}).

