\subsection{Exampville transfer tests: initial validation before Queensland}
Before the Queensland experiments, the framework is exercised end-to-end on an independent data source: Exampville, the synthetic town documented in the discrete-choice teaching literature \citep{LarchExampvilleDocs} (5,000 households, 12,349 persons, 20,739 tours, 40 zones, OMX skims). The full synthetic population is used with no subsampling. This tests portability (same code, different datasource) and gives a like-for-like model comparison: the published example specifications are the ``fitted normally'' comparator, and our searched specifications are the alternative. All Exampville artifacts live in an isolated run directory (models, metrics, mapped frames, skim copy, logs); the shared saved-data store and all Queensland run trees are never touched, so the two experimental streams cannot mix.

Schema mapping is one-to-one (Table~\ref{tab:exampville_map}): household income, vehicles, size, and workers; person age (licence derived as age $\ge 16$, matching the example's DA availability rule) and worker status; tour destination, mode (identical 1--5 coding), and purpose (work vs.\ non-work, routed to the work/non-mandatory segments); skims mapped to canonical names with PM reusing AM and bike distance derived from bike time; employment mapped to size-term attractors. Coverage by stage: Stage~0 CDAP (labels derived in-pipeline from tour counts via the standard observed-pattern rule), Stage~1 frequency (work and consolidated non-mandatory counts derived from tours; person types from worker/age rules, with no school detail so no education or SchoolChild cells), and Stage~5 mode plus destination. Untestable here by construction: stages~2--4 (no diary times or durations), 5b (no intermediate stops), schedule conditioning (no times), and safety terms. Stage-5 estimation runs without schedule times; application uses uniform AM-peak placement; both documented in the adapter.

