\subsection{Prototype-MTC transfer test: real-world prototype validation}
A second, complementary portability check runs on ActivitySim's canonical Bay Area prototype, \texttt{prototype\_mtc} \citep{ActivitySimConsortium,ActivitySimPrototypeMTC} --- real prototype geography and network (TAZ zones, OMX skims) rather than a synthetic town, with ActivitySim's own regress-test outputs as the comparator instead of documentation statistics. The adapter synthesises tours from regress trips grouped by tour identifiers (no tour table ships with the prototype); tour mode follows the main-mode rule (transit $>$ DA $>$ SR $>$ bike $>$ walk), with walk-suffixed legs read as transit access per ActivitySim convention and bare walk as walk. Income maps to 1--14 bands on fixed log-\$ edges; worker status derives from observed work intensity (HOURS/WEEKS/EARNS, full-time at $\ge$30h) rather than the vintage-dependent ptype code; school attendance, licence (age $\ge 16$), and education/census-bridge fields follow the Exampville-adapter rules. Missing prototype columns are documented, not imputed: Primary/Secondary/retail/EATOUT/Entertainment enter as zero (weakening non-mandatory attractors by construction), PM skims reuse AM where absent, and application uses uniform AM-peak placement (STARTIME 480 / ENDTIME 540) since the prototype carries no diary times. The corrected-adapter mapped universe is 9{,}806 trips (3{,}498 work, 979 education, 5{,}329 non-mandatory) from 8{,}212 persons across 25 TAZ zones. Three arms run on identical data: (M0) the ActivitySim regress reference outputs with no fitting; (M1) the pipeline default ActivitySim-style specification; (M2) the GA-searched specification (small budget: population 12, 10 generations). All artifacts live in an isolated run directory; the shared saved-data store and all Queensland run trees are never touched. Out of scope by construction, as with Exampville: stages~2--4, 5b, schedule conditioning, and safety terms.

The M0 reference is established on the corrected run ($n=9{,}806$ trips): walk 57.8\% / transit 34.6\% / bike 3.2\% / shared-ride 2.5\% / drive-alone 1.9\%, mean network trip length 0.91\,km, and destination top-10 zones \{7, 9, 16, 5, 2, 11, 13, 12, 8, 4\} (\Cref{tab:mtc_transfer}). M1 estimates cleanly on all three segments; M2's search returns failure fitness so M2 falls back to the default specification and its metrics coincide with M1 --- consequence, not coincidence: with the search failed there is no searched alternative to compare, and the fallback is reported rather than hidden. Recorded likelihoods and $\rho^2$ are absent from the metrics artifact (null) for both arms, so the comparison rests on aggregate recovery: destination-share MAE $0.0176$, top-10 overlap $0.43$, trip-length MAE $0.52$\,km ($n_{\mathrm{pred}}=24{,}714$). Together the two transfer tests bracket portability: Exampville exercises the harness against documented statistics on synthetic data, while prototype-MTC exercises it against real prototype geography with the platform's own reference outputs as ground truth.

