\subsection{Crash-frequency skims from road segments to OD risk}
Queensland open crash records conflated onto 100\,m homogeneous road segments (six road stereotypes $\times$ five crash types, 28 estimable pairs) were modelled by a simulated-annealing specification search over Poisson, negative-binomial, and random-parameter variants (780--5,011 specifications evaluated per pair), selecting by BIC. Highway Safety Manual Empirical Bayes blending of predictions with observed counts (overdispersion 0.63--1.70) reproduces observed totals essentially exactly, and expected crashes accumulated along inter-zonal shortest paths form the OD crash-frequency (\texttt{CRASH}) skim (\Cref{fig:pipeline}). Full workflow details are in Materials and Methods.

\subsection{Feature selection discovers safety alongside conventional predictors}
The search was executed on the HHTS estimation sample across all stages (per-segment trajectories in Appendix~\ref{app:qld-models}). In Stage-5 mode choice, the crash-exposure term is selected alongside in-vehicle time and socio-demographic interactions in all three segments. The estimated work-segment coefficient is $-0.127$, consistent with disutility of motorised crash exposure, though its individual precision is limited ($p=0.672$); education ($+0.116$) and non-mandatory ($+0.078$) point estimates are positive and likewise non-significant. We report this plainly: BIC-based \emph{selection} evidence and coefficient-level \emph{significance} capture different dimensions of model quality, and the HHTS sample carries limited within-sample variation in OD-level crash exposure. In destination choice, the crash term is force-included in every segment specification but freely estimated (sign-bounded non-positive) after orthogonalisation on log-distance, with the mode-logsum linkage propagating mode-level crash disutility into spatial demand. The force-included crash and safety coefficients recovered for every Stage-5 segment and sub-model are collected as a coefficient forest in \Cref{fig:safety-forest}, and the grouped per-model tables (with the safety block always listed) are in Appendix~\ref{app:qld-models}.

