\subsection{Post-selection inference: search-aware uncertainty}
\label{sec:psresults}

The post-selection procedures defined above are applied to every published fit. Three questions are asked and answered separately. Are the selected terms real? Are the coefficients the search reports correctly located and scaled? Does the safety claim survive any of it?

\paragraph{Selection frequency and stability.}
\Cref{tab:ps} reports, for the key terms of the published specification, the selection frequency $\hat q_j$ from PS-III and the perturbation-stability score from PS-I/PS-IV, alongside the in-sample $t$-ratio where the dead-term audit of the previous section provides one. The three answers are allowed to disagree, and where they do the disagreement is the finding. The search-versus-audit contrast is exactly the signature the dead-term audit flagged: \texttt{c\_dist\_sq} in education-destination has a strong in-sample $t$ ($12.1$) yet is retained by \emph{none} of the 20 bootstrap searches, while the force-included crash term is retained in every search of every segment ($\hat q=1.00$) --- and the dead terms behave as dead (\texttt{acdc\_aggl\_leisure} $\hat q=0.05$). Stability disaggregates further: the two split-sample halves of PS-I agree perfectly for education ($1.000$) yet disagree with the published specification ($0.176$), while the search-aware bootstrap overlap against the published specification (PS-III, mean Jaccard) is $0.452$ (work), $0.159$ (education), $0.483$ (non-mandatory), with PS-IV subsample-neighbourhood stability $0.500/0.176/0.481$ --- so the education segment's searched specification is the least stable under search-aware resampling.

