\paragraph{The search-inflated threshold.}
Under PS-III the empirical distribution of $|\hat t^{(b)}_j|$ across bootstrap replications is compared with the nominal $z_{1-\alpha/2}$ used inside the search. The ratio of the two critical values is reported per segment; it is the width factor by which every searched coefficient's confidence interval must be inflated once the search is taken into account. Where the ratio is close to one, the search is not in fact selecting on noise, and the nominal intervals stand; where it is well above one, the searched intervals are widened and the nominal ones are shown struck through in the reported table. 

\paragraph{What survives.}
The force-included safety terms are exempt from all of the above by construction, and their test is a single pre-specified restriction rather than a comparison of a chosen against an unchosen specification,
\begin{equation}
\Lambda_{\mathrm{crash}}=-2\bigl(\ell_{\mathcal{M}_0}-\ell_{\mathcal{M}_S}\bigr)\sim\chi^2_1,
\qquad c_{\mathrm{crash}}=0\ \text{under}\ \mathcal{M}_0,
\label{eq:crash-lr}
\end{equation}
