\subsection{Safety effects beyond penalised fit}
\label{sec:safetyeffects}

A reduction in penalised fit is the weakest possible evidence that safety matters: $\Delta\mathrm{BIC}$ can be produced by a term that changes no choice. The safety claim is therefore evaluated on quantities that are \emph{not} fit criteria at all --- five families of them, all computed by applying the \emph{unmodified} fitted safety model $\mathcal{M}_S$ and its baseline counterpart $\mathcal{M}_0$ to the full synthetic population, so that any difference is a behavioural difference rather than an estimation artefact.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{S\arabic*.}, leftmargin=2.5em]
  \item \textbf{Mode substitution.} Mode shares by segment under $\mathcal{M}_S$ and $\mathcal{M}_0$, with the active-mode share (walk $+$ bike) reported first because it is the quantity safety policy targets.
  \item \textbf{Spatial substitution.} The mean expected-crash exposure of the \emph{chosen} destination, $\sum_t \mathrm{CRASH}_{o_t j_t}/\sum_t 1$, and the distribution of the destination distance. This detects the logsum-mediated pathway directly: a safety-aware destination layer must move tours toward low-crash zones even when the mode is unchanged.
  \item \textbf{Accessibility.} Active-mode opportunity accessibility, using the Hansen-type logsum of \eqref{eq:acc-logsum} restricted to walk and bike alternatives, evaluated at the destination level; a crash-penalised model that does not change this has not changed behaviour.
  \item \textbf{Distributional response.} The same mode-substitution contrast disaggregated by the risk-sensitive groups named in the Introduction (children and teenagers, older persons, low-income persons), which tests the paper's central equity claim rather than the average effect.
  \item \textbf{Induced-demand counterfactual.} A crash-reduction scenario applied to the skim, with all fitted coefficients held fixed.
\end{enumerate}

The counterfactual rescales the published OD crash skim and re-applies the fitted model, so it prices what an intervention to the \emph{network} would do to behaviour at current preferences:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{CRASH}^{(1-\delta)}_{jk}=(1-\delta)\,\mathrm{CRASH}_{jk},\qquad
\delta=0.2,
\label{eq:cf}
\end{equation}
and the induced active-travel demand elasticity is the proportional change in active-mode share per unit crash reduction,
\begin{equation}
\eta_{\mathrm{act}}(\delta)=
\frac{\overline{s}^{\,\mathrm{act}}_{\mathrm{cf}}-\overline{s}^{\,\mathrm{act}}_{\mathcal{M}_S}}
{\overline{s}^{\,\mathrm{act}}_{\mathcal{M}_S}\cdot\delta},
\qquad
\overline{s}^{\,\mathrm{act}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{U}|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{U}}\mathbf{1}\{m_t\in\{\mathrm{WK},\mathrm{BK}\}\},
\label{eq:elasticity}
\end{equation}
with $\overline{s}^{act}_{cf}$ evaluated under \eqref{eq:cf}. The force-included crash terms make this a counterfactual on a pre-specified parameter, not a counterfactual on a selected one, which is the same argument that makes the likelihood-ratio test of \eqref{eq:crash-lr} valid. Finally, the logsum-mediated and direct pathways are separated by re-running the counterfactual with the destination crash term switched off while the mode crash term is kept, and vice versa; the difference between the two partial counterfactuals attributes the response to the logsum linkage \eqref{eq:m5m-ls} rather than to the direct zone penalty \eqref{eq:dest}.

\paragraph{Sensitivity design; uncertainty and equity reporting.}
Three sensitivity families are run against every headline S1--S5 result, so no safety conclusion rests on a single metric choice. (i)~\emph{Crash-metric variants}: the skim enters utilities in log-total form by default, with the per-kilometre and per-exposure variants already supplied to the search as alternative functional forms (noted above); a severity-weighted variant joins them as an explicit sensitivity pass over the same fitted models, and each S-quantity is recomputed under every kept variant.
\StatusTodo{Wire the severity-weighted and per-exposure skim variants into the counterfactual pass and report them alongside the headline S1--S5 rows.} (ii)~\emph{Forced-in vs.\ excluded}: the $\mathcal{M}_S$-versus-$\mathcal{M}_0$ contrast is exactly the forced-in-versus-excluded axis, and the LR statistics \eqref{eq:crash-lr} adjudicate the force. (iii)~\emph{Selection-penalty and $\alpha$ variants}: the significance-constraint level and the exact-penalty weight are varied to check that the selected safety-inclusive specification is not an artefact of one configuration. Uncertainty bounds for the S1--S5 quantities are the intervals induced by the PS-III search-aware bootstrap \eqref{eq:psboot} --- each replication's refit coefficients are propagated through the identical application pass --- and the equity dimension is the S4 distributional breakdown by children and teenagers, older persons, and low-income persons, reported with the same bounds, so distributional claims are made only where the disaggregated contrast clears its interval.

