\section{Discussion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Treating safety as a behavioural determinant rather than a network outcome lets the framework represent active-mode suppression, crash-prone destination avoidance through the logsum mechanism, and route-level exposure sensitivity that conventional models cannot. For choice modellers, three lessons are central. First, the mode-logsum linkage is the mechanism by which a mode-level attribute (crash exposure) reshapes spatial demand without re-estimating destination preferences from scratch, and the latent-class arm shows the same mechanism carrying class-specific heterogeneity through the class-averaged logsum into the destination layer. Second, universal choice sets are strongly rejected as a behavioural assumption once schedule conditioning, captivity, and sampling are tested: context-sensitive, individualised sets improve fit and realism, especially for destination and schedule choices. Third, a specification found by search cannot be certified by the same data that found it: every searched quantity here is reported with split-sample and search-aware uncertainty, the force-included safety terms and the counterfactual comparisons are exempt and are the quantities the safety claims rest on, and penalised fit is never used on its own as evidence of a genuine effect --- exactly the separation advocated in \citet{Hess2026Significance}. The evolutionary search confirms crash exposure belongs in the specification conversation, even though HHTS-sample precision on its coefficient is weak --- a finding that itself motivates perception-enriched data collection rather than analyst assertion.

Limitations are stated openly. The validation protocol of this paper is held out by construction, but several of its passes are still running and are marked as pending where their numbers have not yet landed; no selection-side figure is presented as validation in the meantime. The destination crash coefficient is force-\emph{included} in every specification but freely \emph{estimated} (sign-bounded non-positive, orthogonalised on log-distance so it identifies crash sensitivity independent of trip length); its magnitude should still not be interpreted as a willingness-to-pay estimate --- read instead as a sign-and-precision statement, following the value-of-travel-time reporting convention of \citet{Daly2020VTT} --- given the HHTS-sample precision limits above (decision versus analyst error and nonlinearity \citep{BekkerGrobRoseBliemer2013Closer,Hess2026Significance}). The OD crash skim accumulates exposure along single shortest paths with no route-choice model, and expresses totals rather than rates, making the negative crash coefficients conservative conditional on distance. Planned extensions include dynamic safety skims with assignment feedback (integrated ABM--assignment convergence \citep{ABMDTAConvergence2024}), the MDCEV episode allocator as default Stage-3 estimator, latent mode availability, and multi-day schedules (multi-day location choice \citep{Arentze2013MultiDay}).

